# sprint_sec_fs_native.oot — SEC hostile audit of native fs_* + sh mkdir/copy/clean
# Date: 2026-08-14
# Role: SEC. This file only. No product edit. Did not touch fs.oo / sh.oo / chs_rt_fs.c.
# Marker: post-DE10 partial native land (mkdir_p + copy_tree); clean still shell
# Surfaces: /home/jeryd/Projects/openOODA/ooda/std/os/fs.oo
#           /home/jeryd/Projects/openOODA/ooda/std/sh.oo
#           /home/jeryd/Projects/openOODA/ooda/runtime/chs_rt_fs.c
#           /home/jeryd/Projects/openOODA/ooda/std/os/process.oo
#           /home/jeryd/Projects/openOODA/ooda/oodac/check_cap_util.oo
# Hunt: mkdir_p ignore fs_mkdir Err (TOCTOU / file-in-the-way)
#       copy recursion
#       clean still rm -rf injection if arg gate bypassed
#       OODA_FS_WRITEDIR still required by runtime
# Method: static re-read. No live rm -rf / mkdir-race probe this turn.
#         Do not claim secure. Do not green the native wave.
## VERDICT
**Not sealed. Partial move only. Residual honesty.**

`sh_mkdir_p` is now `&FsWriteCap` → `fs_mkdir_p`.
`sh_copy` is now `&FsReadCap` + `&FsWriteCap` → `fs_copy_tree`.
Those two left `sh -c`. That is real.

They did not become a seal.

`fs_mkdir_p` **never returns Err**. Every `fs_mkdir` Result is `let _ =`.
Writedir deny, EEXIST-as-file, ENOTDIR, EACCES, and the last-component
fail are all discarded. Callers see `Ok(void)`. `fs_copy_tree`'s
`if mk.is_err()` is dead.

`fs_copy_tree` follows `stat`/`opendir` (no `lstat`). A symlink in
`src` walks any readable host tree. Recursion has no depth cap and
no cycle stop. A link back to `src` is unbounded. `fs_is_dir` clamps
to 1024 bytes; writes clamp to `PATH_MAX`. Those are not the same
path.

`sh_clean` is still `ProcessCap` + `process_exec("rm -rf " + dir)`.
`OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` does not apply. The char gate is not argv. Space
and `-` are in-class. `--no-preserve-root /` **passes** `sh_arg_ok`.
If the gate is bypassed, `;` `|` `$()` are the old RCE. Even with
the gate, GNU `rm` can take a second operand or a wipe flag.

Runtime mutate still **requires** `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` (unset / empty
/ `/` deny). Native mkdir/write/remove/link/symlink all call
`oo_process_policy_getenv("OODA_FS_WRITEDIR")`. The facade hides
that fail: `fs_mkdir_p` swallows the deny and still returns Ok.
`sh_clean` never consults the overlay.

Do not say native fs replaced shell. Do not say mkdir_p is mkdir -p.
Do not say copy is cp -a. Do not say clean is gated.

---
WHAT LANDED (do not over-claim)
---

Re-read this turn.

| Op | Tip claim | Live | Cap |
|----|-----------|------|-----|
| `sh_mkdir_p` | native | `return fs_mkdir_p(fw, dir)` after `sh_arg_ok` | **FsWriteCap** |
| `sh_copy` | native | `return fs_copy_tree(fr, fw, src, dst)` after gate | **FsReadCap + FsWriteCap** |
| `sh_clean` | native residual | `process_exec(p, "rm -rf " + dir)` | **ProcessCap** |
| `fs_mkdir_p` | prefixes | walk `/` + `let _ = fs_mkdir` + **always Ok** | Fw |
| `fs_copy_file` | one file | `read_file` whole → `write_file` | Fr+Fw |
| `fs_copy_tree` | walk + mkdir + copy | `fs_is_dir` then recurse `src+"/"+name` | Fr+Fw |

`sh.oo:15` still says "git/tar/chmod/clean still shell after sh_arg_ok
(no rmdir builtin)". That comment is true. mkdir/copy comments in the
header (beats L8) are stale: they still list mkdir/copy as path mutators
next to chmod as if they were the same class.

No `remove_tree` / `rmdir` builtin. That is why clean stayed shell.

---
HUNT 1 — fs_mkdir_p ignores fs_mkdir Err (TOCTOU / file-in-the-way)
---

Proof of shape (`std/os/fs.oo:102-135`):

    // mkdir every prefix. Existing dirs: fs_mkdir may Err; we continue.
    pub fn fs_mkdir_p(fw: &FsWriteCap, path: String) -> Result[Void, String]
    {
        ...
                    let _ = fs_mkdir(fw, acc);
        ...
            let _ = fs_mkdir(fw, acc);
        ...
        return Ok(void);
    }

There is **no** `is_err` branch. There is **no** `fs_is_dir` confirm
after a failed mkdir. EEXIST-as-dir and EEXIST-as-file are the same
discard. Writedir deny is the same discard.

Runtime `oo_fs_mkdir` (`chs_rt_fs.c:133-141`):

    require_fswrite
    OODA_FS_WRITEDIR fail-closed (unset / empty / `/` / not under)
    mkdir(cpath, 0777) == 0  → Ok
    else                     → Err("fs_mkdir failed")

EEXIST is **not** success. POSIX `mkdir(2)` fails if the last
component exists, dir or not. The .oo layer treats that Err as
"probably already a dir." That is the mkdir -p happy path **and**
the file-in-the-way lie.

### File in the way
1. Prefix is a **regular file**.
   `mkdir(prefix)` → EEXIST → discarded.
   `mkdir(prefix/child)` → ENOTDIR → discarded.
   `fs_mkdir_p` → **Ok(void)**.
   Dest tree does not exist.

2. Prefix is a **symlink to a file**.
   Same. `stat` later (copy) may disagree with what mkdir created.

3. Last component is a **file**, caller wanted a directory.
   `mkdir` fails. `fs_mkdir_p` still Ok. `fs_copy_tree` then writes
   `dst/name` via `write_file`. `writedir_open_trunc` does
   `open(parent, O_DIRECTORY)` — parent is a file → fail. Copy of
   children fails **later**, or returns Ok on an empty dirent list
   if the src walk is also confused. The mkdir_p Result already lied.

### TOCTOU (check vs create)
`oo_fs_mkdir` is not `mkdirat`. Order is:

    path_under_writedir(cpath)     // realpath of path, or parent
    mkdir(cpath, 0777)             // follows symlink components

No `O_NOFOLLOW`. No dirfd pin. Contrast `write_file`, which uses
`openat(..., O_NOFOLLOW)` after parent realpath (`chs_rt_fs.c:33-39`).

Race:

    path = $WRITEDIR/subdir/new
    parent $WRITEDIR/subdir is a real directory → allow
    attacker replaces subdir with a symlink to /etc
    mkdir("$WRITEDIR/subdir/new") follows the link → /etc/new

`path_under_writedir` for a **new** leaf uses `realpath(parent)`.
That is a TOCTOU window, not a seal. Same class as T3 W8 (parent
dentry rename) plus a follow-symlink create that write_file
partially closed at the leaf.

If the **new** path's parent is already a symlink whose realpath
leaves writedir, the check denies. That is the static case. The
swap-after-check case is open.

### Swallowing the overlay
`fs_mkdir("/tmp/outside")` with `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR=/tmp/wd` is Err.
`fs_mkdir_p("/tmp/outside/a/b")` is Ok. Every prefix mkdir denied.
Caller proceeds as if the tree exists.

`fs_mkdir_p("rel/path")` is Ok. Runtime requires `path[0]=='/'`
(`path_under_writedir` L26). Relative mkdir is always denied. Facade
hides it.

`fs_mkdir_p("/")` skips (`acc != "/"`). Ok. No-op.

`fs_copy_tree` (`fs.oo:155-156`):

    let mk = fs_mkdir_p(fw, dst);
    if mk.is_err() { return mk; }

Dead. mkdir_p cannot take that branch.

| Case | mkdir(2) / policy | mkdir_p Result | Honest? |
|------|-------------------|----------------|---------|
| new dir under writedir | 0 | Ok | yes |
| existing dir | EEXIST | Ok | intended mkdir -p |
| existing **file** | EEXIST | **Ok** | **NO** |
| parent is file | ENOTDIR | **Ok** | **NO** |
| outside writedir | policy Err | **Ok** | **NO** |
| relative path | policy Err | **Ok** | **NO** |
| parent swapped to symlink | mkdir follows | Ok if create works | **TOCTOU** |
| EACCES / EROFS | Err | **Ok** | **NO** |

Fix that would be honesty, not this turn: on mkdir Err, `lstat`;
if not `S_ISDIR`, return that Err. Never discard writedir deny.
Use `mkdirat` + `O_NOFOLLOW` / `AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW`. Do not
claim mkdir -p until EEXIST-dir is distinguished.

